# Everything is LF, in the repository and in the working tree.
#
# This is not a style preference. server/db.js checksums each migration
# file's bytes and refuses to boot if one differs from the checksum
# recorded when it ran — which is the right guard, because silently
# re-running an edited migration is how a database stops matching the
# code that reads it.
#
# On Windows, core.autocrlf=true checks these files out with CRLF. That
# is invisible while deploys go through GitHub, which serves LF. It is
# not invisible to `railway up`, which uploads the working tree: every
# migration checksum changed at once and production crashed on boot
# with "001_init.sql changed after it ran".
#
# eol=lf makes the working tree match what is committed, so the bytes
# are the same however the code gets to the server.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never touched, never normalised.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.bin   binary
*.db    binary
